out.push(S('Unit 1 check · A  Grammar and vocabulary'));
out.push(A('**1 b** have worked   **2 b** am working   **3 b** don\'t understand   **4 a** How did   **5 b** does   **6 b** as   **7 a** tends   **8 a** like'));
out.push(S('Unit 1 check · B  Listening (Track 1.15)'));
out.push(A('**1 B** nine years   **2 B** writing every fault down   **3 A** it is where the clever quiet one sits   **4 B** she was deciding   **5 A** understanding English   **6 A** to be told the same day   **7 A** she will not mind silence'));
out.push(NOTE('Part 1 at Preliminary is seven unrelated extracts and the skill is **not** losing the question between them. Play it twice, as the rubric says, and pause for five seconds between extracts on the first play only. **6** is the one students get wrong, because the extract says what the speaker is **not** asking for immediately after what she is asking for, and anybody listening for the last thing said will choose **B**.'));
out.push(S('Unit 1 check · C  Writing (sample, 134 words)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['There are nine of us in the department and the division that matters is not the one on the chart. It is the five who were here before the move and the four who came after, and the first five still describe where things are by reference to a building we left in 2021. The list says Rula is "the one to ask", which is true and makes her sound like a service. What is actually true is that she has answered the same eleven questions for six years because nobody has ever written them down, and if you write her answers down you will be the first. About me it says I am "easy to work with", which is somebody being kind: I agree to things in the room and raise them afterwards by email, and I know it. What the list cannot tell you is which two days of the month everybody is unreachable, and it is the two before the invoices go out. If you start in November, ask Rula for her eleven questions on the first morning and write them down.']));
out.push(S('"I can…" checklist'));
out.push(A('Students tick for themselves. Two lines matter more than the others at this stage and are worth naming: **keeping state verbs out of the continuous**, which is the error that will survive the whole level if it is not fixed in the first fortnight, and **hedging without losing the information**, which is the one thing in this unit that is not grammar and cannot be tested in the check. Come back to both in Unit 4.'));
out.push(sp());
